@page "/portfolio"
@inject CharacterSession Session
@inject ReplayLibrary Replays

<PageTitle>How this was built — Prowlers &amp; Paragons</PageTitle>

<h1>How this was built</h1>
<p class="muted">
    The other half of this site is a tool for use at a table. This half is about the tool: what
    it does that is hard, and two ways to see it doing that without building a character first.
</p>

@* **This page is why the split exists.** Both of the things below are demonstrations — they
   show somebody what the engine can do — and both used to sit in the middle of the character
   generator, where somebody actually building a character had to walk past them. A recording of
   a stranger's conversation and a pair of pre-made characters are not steps in making your own. *@

<Panel Title="Recorded conversations">
    <p class="small muted">
        Conversations in which somebody described a character in ordinary words and got a costed,
        checked one back. They are recordings — each was said once and plays back exactly as it
        was. <b>The figures beside them are not recorded:</b> every Hero Point cost and every
        finding is worked out in your browser, from the character the recording carries, as you
        reveal it. If the rules and the recording ever disagreed, you would watch it happen.
    </p>

    @if (Replays.Problem is not null)
    {
        <p class="small">The recordings could not be loaded. What went wrong: @Replays.Problem</p>
    }
    else
    {
        <div class="inline">
            <NavLink class="btn primary" href="portfolio/replay">Watch one being built</NavLink>
            <span class="small muted">@Replays.Conversations.Count recordings</span>
        </div>
    }
</Panel>

<Panel Title="Two finished characters" Aside="Replaces anything already on the sheet">
    <p class="small muted">
        Two characters written for this tool, filling in every section a sheet has. The quickest
        way to see what you get at the end — and what a printed sheet looks like — without
        spending twenty minutes building one first. Both are legal Standard-tier characters, and
        there is a test that says so.
    </p>

    @* Asks first, and only when there is something to lose. Loading a sample replaces the
       character outright, and the character is kept in this browser between visits — so this
       button can destroy work written down nowhere else, with no undo behind it. On an empty
       sheet it acts on one click, which is the visit where somebody is most likely to want a
       sample and least likely to have anything at stake. *@
    <div class="inline">
        @if (_pending is { } pending)
        {
            <button type="button" class="btn danger" @onclick="() => Do(pending)">
                Yes, @pending.Verb
            </button>
            <button type="button" class="btn" @onclick="() => _pending = null">
                Keep this character
            </button>
        }
        else
        {
            <button type="button" class="btn" @onclick="() => Ask(Load(SheetMode.Hero))">
                Load a Hero — Ninth Precinct
            </button>
            <button type="button" class="btn" @onclick="() => Ask(Load(SheetMode.Villain))">
                Load a Villain — The Quiet Hour
            </button>
        }
        @if (_loaded is not null)
        {
            <span class="small muted">Loaded <b>@_loaded</b> — it is on the sheet now.</span>
        }
    </div>

    @if (_loaded is not null)
    {
        <div class="inline spaced">
            <NavLink class="btn primary" href="review">See the sheet</NavLink>
            <NavLink class="btn" href="">Back to building</NavLink>
        </div>
    }
</Panel>

@code {
    private string? _loaded;
    private Replacement? _pending;

    /// <summary>Something that replaces the character, and what to call it when asking.</summary>
    private sealed record Replacement(string Verb, Func<Task> Run);

    /// <summary>
    /// <b>The palette is not pushed here.</b> It lives on the character, and the layout applies it
    /// on the render after any change of character — so a sample brings its own colours with it.
    /// A push here as well would be a second route to the same thing, and a second route is where
    /// the two get to disagree.
    /// </summary>
    private Replacement Load(SheetMode mode) => new(
        mode == SheetMode.Hero ? "load the Hero" : "load the Villain",
        () =>
        {
            Session.LoadSample(mode);
            _loaded = Session.Sheet.Name;
            return Task.CompletedTask;
        });

    /// <summary>Asks first, unless the sheet is empty and there is nothing to ask about.</summary>
    private async Task Ask(Replacement replacement)
    {
        if (Session.HasSomethingToLose) _pending = replacement;
        else await replacement.Run();
    }

    private async Task Do(Replacement replacement)
    {
        _pending = null;
        await replacement.Run();
    }
}
